kai_gazebo ships worlds built on terrain models from other projects:

- Sydney International Regatta Centre and Nathan Benderson Park: models
  from the Virtual RobotX competition (osrf/vrx), Apache 2.0, fetched from Fuel
  (https://app.gazebosim.org/openrobotics) at run time.
- Sand Island: the site and its optional seabed are models on Gazebo Fuel
  (https://app.gazebosim.org/honuRobotics: sand_island and
  sand_island_seabed), fetched at run time, and its water
  (models/water_surface_sand_island) ships here, all by Honu Robotics under
  the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License. They are
  built from:
  - OpenStreetMap: the shoreline, land cover, buildings, bridges, piers,
    breakwaters, cranes, trees and seamarks. Map data (c) OpenStreetMap
    contributors, available under the Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0,
    https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright. The meshes are a Produced Work
    of that data.
  - Sentinel-2 cloudless (https://s2maps.eu) by EOX IT Services GmbH
    (contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data 2016), under the Creative
    Commons Attribution 4.0 International License: the colour of the ground
    and of the far field in the sky.
  - The AWS Terrain Tiles (https://registry.opendata.aws/terrain-tiles/),
    Mapzen's Terrarium elevation: SRTM, USGS 3DEP and NED, GMTED2010, ETOPO1
    and others, see https://github.com/tilezen/joerd/blob/master/docs/attribution.md.
  - Poly Haven's sunflowers_puresky (https://polyhaven.com), CC0, turned
    and with the far field painted in.
  - The seabed: NOAA NCEI's Continuously Updated Digital Elevation Model
    (CUDEM), 1/9 arc second topobathymetric tiles, Hawaii (2021),
    https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/coastal-elevation-models, a work of
    the US Government, in the public domain.
- La Spezia marina: the Virtual Ocean Robotics Challenge (VORC) terrain mesh
  and texture (osrf/vorc, vorc_gazebo/models/marina), Apache 2.0, fetched
  at build time from a pinned commit.
